+# 14 — Dev/SRE Toolbox
+
+Audience: Developers, SRE. Status: implemented (MVP).
+
+## How to read this document (Diátaxis)
+
+This document deliberately keeps the four Diátaxis modes in separate, linkable
+sections so each can be consumed on its own:
+
+| Mode | Section |
+|------|---------|
+| Tutorial | [Try it: a guided walkthrough](#try-it-a-guided-walkthrough) |
+| How-to | [Adding a new tool](#adding-a-new-tool) |
+| Reference | [Endpoints](#endpoints), [Result contract](#result-contract), [Configuration and limits](#configuration-and-limits), [Kubernetes checks](#kubernetes-checks--what-they-do-and-do-not-do) |
+| Explanation | [What it is](#what-it-is), [Architecture note](#architecture-note) |
+
+The reference sections are the ones linked from the OpenAPI operation descriptions.
+
+## What it is
+
+The Dev/SRE Toolbox is a modular feature **inside the existing FastAPI application**
+(no separate service, no new infrastructure). It exposes a small catalogue of
+technical utilities over the API and returns a uniform, typed result.
+
+Three tools ship in this MVP:
+
+| Tool | Version | Purpose |
+|------|---------|---------|
+| `json.validate` | 1.0.0 | Syntactic validation of a JSON document supplied as text; returns the parsed value or the error position. |
+| `text.diff` | 1.0.0 | Unified diff between two strings, with a configurable number of context lines. |
+| `k8s.manifest.inspect` | 1.0.0 | Static, offline checks on Kubernetes YAML manifests (Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet). |
+
+All checks are **local, bounded and synchronous**. No network call, no Kubernetes
+cluster, no external credential, no queue, no persistence.
+
+## Endpoints
+
+The prefix follows the repository convention (a single `/api` prefix; the app has no
+versioned `/v1` segment anywhere), so the mandate's `/api/v1/toolbox` is mounted as
+`/api/toolbox`.
+
+Both endpoints require the application's existing HTTP Basic Auth
+(`app.auth.verify_credentials`, the same dependency used by `/api/debug/*` and
+`/api/mgmt/threaddump`). **Every example below carries the credentials**, so it works
+as written; without them the call returns `401`.
+
+### `GET /api/toolbox/tools`
+
+Returns the catalogue: identifier, description, version and JSON input schema of every
+tool, sorted by identifier (deterministic order).
+
+bash
+curl -s -u admin:password http://localhost:8000/api/toolbox/tools | python3 -m json.tool
+
+
+
+{
+  "tools": [
+    {
+      "name": "json.validate",
+      "description": "Validate the syntax of a JSON document supplied as text ...",
+      "version": "1.0.0",
+      "input_schema": {"properties": {"text": {"title": "Text", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["text"], "title": "JsonValidateInput", "type": "object"}
+    },
+    {
+      "name": "k8s.manifest.inspect",
+      "description": "Run static, offline checks on a Kubernetes YAML manifest ...",
+      "version": "1.0.0",
+      "input_schema": {"properties": {"manifest": {"title": "Manifest", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["manifest"], "title": "K8sManifestInspectInput", "type": "object"}
+    },
+    {
+      "name": "text.diff",
+      "description": "Return a unified diff between two strings ...",
+      "version": "1.0.0",
+      "input_schema": {"properties": {"left": {"title": "Left", "type": "string"}, "right": {"title": "Right", "type": "string"}, "context_lines": {"default": 3, "minimum": 0, "title": "Context Lines", "type": "integer"}}, "required": ["left", "right"], "title": "TextDiffInput", "type": "object"}
+    }
+  ]
+}
+
+
+### `POST /api/toolbox/runs`
+
+Runs one tool and returns HTTP 200 with the result and a `run_id`.
+
+bash
+curl -s -u admin:password -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/toolbox/runs \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"tool": "json.validate", "input": {"text": "{\"a\": 1}"}}' | python3 -m json.tool
+
+
+
+{
+  "run_id": "0f0a1c1e-6f1a-4a1e-9a1e-2b0f0a1c1e6f",
+  "tool": "json.validate",
+  "tool_version": "1.0.0",
+  "status": "completed",
+  "outcome": "pass",
+  "summary": {"info": 0, "warning": 0, "error": 0},
+  "findings": [],
+  "output": {"valid": true, "value": {"a": 1}},
+  "duration_ms": 1
+}
+
+
+Invalid JSON is **not** an HTTP error: it is a negative analysis result.
+
+bash
+curl -s -u admin:password -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/toolbox/runs \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"tool": "json.validate", "input": {"text": "{\"a\": }"}}' | python3 -m json.tool
+
+
+json
+{
+  "run_id": "...",
+  "tool": "json.validate",
+  "tool_version": "1.0.0",
+  "status": "completed",
+  "outcome": "fail",
+  "summary": {"info": 0, "warning": 0, "error": 1},
+  "findings": [
+    {
+      "code": "JSON_INVALID",
+      "severity": "error",
+      "message": "Invalid JSON: Expecting value",
+      "location": {"document_index": null, "resource_kind": null, "resource_name": null, "path": "line 1, column 7"},
+      "suggestion": "Fix the syntax at the reported line and column and run the tool again."
+    }
+  ],
+  "output": {"valid": false, "line": 1, "column": 7, "position": 6},
+  "duration_ms": 1
+}
+
+
+`text.diff`:
+
+bash
+curl -s -u admin:password -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/toolbox/runs \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"tool": "text.diff", "input": {"left": "a\nb\nc", "right": "a\nB\nc", "context_lines": 1}}' | python3 -m json.tool
+
+
+
+{
+  "run_id": "...",
+  "tool": "text.diff",
+  "tool_version": "1.0.0",
+  "status": "completed",
+  "outcome": "fail",
+  "summary": {"info": 0, "warning": 0, "error": 0},
+  "findings": [],
+  "output": {"identical": false, "context_lines": 1, "diff": "--- left\n+++ right\n@@ -1,3 +1,3 @@\n a\n-b\n+B\n c"},
+  "duration_ms": 1
+}
+
+
+`k8s.manifest.inspect`:
+
+bash
+curl -s -u admin:password -X POST http://localhost:8000/api/toolbox/runs \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"tool": "k8s.manifest.inspect", "input": {"manifest": "apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: example\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: app\n          image: nginx:latest\n          securityContext:\n            privileged: true\n"}}' | python3 -m json.tool
+
+
+
+{
+  "run_id": "...",
+  "tool": "k8s.manifest.inspect",
+  "tool_version": "1.0.0",
+  "status": "completed",
+  "outcome": "fail",
+  "summary": {"info": 0, "warning": 5, "error": 1},
+  "findings": [
+    {
+      "code": "K8S_PRIVILEGED_CONTAINER",
+      "severity": "error",
+      "message": "Container 'app' is configured as privileged.",
+      "location": {"document_index": 0, "resource_kind": "Deployment", "resource_name": "example", "path": "spec.template.spec.containers[0].securityContext.privileged"},
+      "suggestion": "Disable privileged unless it is explicitly required."
+    }
+  ],
+  "output": {"documents": 1, "resources_inspected": 1, "supported_kinds": ["Deployment", "StatefulSet", "DaemonSet"], "limit_exceeded": false, "findings_total": 6, "findings_returned": 6, "disclaimer": "Static, offline checks only. This is not a complete Kubernetes validation and not a security certification."},
+  "duration_ms": 3
+}
+
+
+## Try it: a guided walkthrough
+
+1. Start the app: `uvicorn app.main:app --port 8000` (or `docker compose up -d`).
+2. List the catalogue with the command in [`GET /api/toolbox/tools`](#get-apitoolboxtools).
+3. Run `json.validate` with a valid document, then with a broken one, and compare the
+   `outcome` field: both return HTTP 200.
+4. Run `text.diff` on two short strings and read the unified diff in `output.diff`.
+5. Run `k8s.manifest.inspect` on the sample manifest above and read the findings.
+
+## Result contract
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|-------|---------|
+| `run_id` | UUID generated per request. Correlation of response, logs and tracing only: it is not persisted and there is no `GET /runs/{id}`. |
+| `tool`, `tool_version` | Identifier and version of the tool that ran. |
+| `status` | Execution status of the run: `completed` or `failed`. |
+| `outcome` | Analysis outcome: `pass`, `fail` or `error`. A finding is never an HTTP error. |
+| `summary` | Number of findings per severity (`info`, `warning`, `error`). |
+| `findings` | List of `{code, severity, message, location, suggestion}`. |
+| `output` | Tool-specific structured output. |
+| `duration_ms` | Wall-clock duration of the analysis. |
+
+Four things are kept distinct:
+
+1. **execution status** (`status`);
+2. **analysis outcome** (`outcome`);
+3. **request validation errors** — FastAPI's own 422 for a body that does not match
+   `ToolRunRequest`, or a 422 with a typed `detail` when `input` does not match the
+   tool's input model;
+4. **unexpected internal errors** — HTTP 500 with `{"detail": "Internal server error"}`,
+   sanitized by the existing `ErrorInjectionMiddleware`; no stack trace, no module path,
+   no exception class name is returned.
+
+Syntactically invalid input (bad JSON, bad YAML) produces a negative analysis result
+(`outcome: "fail"` with a finding), because that is the purpose of the tool. A
+structurally wrong *request* follows the application's validation error handling.
+
+### Error channels (exact status and body)
+
+| Situation | Status | Body |
+|-----------|--------|------|
+| Missing or wrong Basic Auth | `401` | `{"detail": "Not authenticated"}` |
+| Body does not match `ToolRunRequest` | `422` | FastAPI validation envelope |
+| `input` does not match the tool's input model | `422` | `{"detail": {"error": "Invalid tool input", "tool": ..., "errors": [...]}}` |
+| Unknown tool id | `404` | `{"error": "Not found"}` — the app-wide 404 handler rewrites the body, so the caller **cannot** distinguish "unknown tool" from any other 404 by body. |
+| Input or request over a limit | `413` | `{"detail": "<reason with observed and maximum value>"}` |
+| Unexpected internal error | `500` | `{"detail": "Internal server error"}` |
+
+## Configuration and limits
+
+All limits are Pydantic Settings fields in `app/config/settings.py`, driven by
+environment variables like the rest of the configuration.
+
+| Variable | Default | Applies to |
+|----------|---------|------------|
+| `TOOLBOX_ENABLED` | `true` | Mounts `/api/toolbox` in `create_app()`. Set to `false` to remove the routes entirely. |
+| `TOOLBOX_MAX_REQUEST_BYTES` | `1048576` | `Content-Length` of `POST /api/toolbox/runs`. |
+| `TOOLBOX_MAX_INPUT_BYTES` | `262144` | Size of a single text input (`text`, `left`, `right`, `manifest`). |
+| `TOOLBOX_MAX_YAML_DOCUMENTS` | `50` | Number of YAML documents in a manifest. |
+| `TOOLBOX_MAX_YAML_DEPTH` | `32` | Nesting depth of a YAML document. |
+| `TOOLBOX_MAX_FINDINGS` | `200` | Number of findings returned by a tool. |
+| `TOOLBOX_MAX_DIFF_CONTEXT_LINES` | `10` | `context_lines` accepted by `text.diff`. |
+
+> Note: these variables are read from the environment / `.env` like every other
+> setting. They are not yet listed in `.env.example`; add them there when the file is
+> next touched (that file is outside the documentation write scope of this change).
+
+### What happens when a limit is reached — no silent truncation
+
+* **Request body too large** → HTTP **413** with a message stating the observed size and
+  the configured maximum.
+* **Single input too large** → HTTP **413** with the same kind of message.
+* **Too many YAML documents** → HTTP **413**.
+* **YAML document too deep** → HTTP **413**.
+* **`context_lines` above the maximum** → HTTP **413**.
+* **More findings than `TOOLBOX_MAX_FINDINGS`** → the run still returns HTTP 200, but
+  `outcome` is `"error"`, `output.limit_exceeded` is `true`, `output.findings_total`
+  reports how many findings were produced, and the list ends with an explicit
+  `TOOLBOX_FINDINGS_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` finding that says how many were kept. Nothing is
+  dropped silently.
+
+HTTP 413 is used deliberately instead of 400: the application-wide 400 handler in
+`app/main.py` rewrites the body to `{"error": "Bad request"}` and would hide the reason.
+
+## Kubernetes checks — what they do and do not do
+
+`k8s.manifest.inspect` parses the manifest with `yaml.safe_load_all` through a
+`SafeLoader` subclass that additionally **rejects YAML aliases** (a small document can
+otherwise expand into a huge object graph). It never contacts a cluster, never reads a
+file or a URL, and never constructs arbitrary Python objects.
+
+Supported kinds: **Deployment, StatefulSet, DaemonSet**. Any other kind produces an
+`info` finding (`K8S_UNSUPPORTED_KIND`) and is not inspected.
+
+Checks, per container:
+
+| Code | Severity | Condition |
+|------|----------|-----------|
+| `K8S_CONTAINER_MISSING_REQUESTS` | warning | no `resources.requests` |
+| `K8S_CONTAINER_MISSING_LIMITS` | warning | no `resources.limits` |
+| `K8S_MISSING_READINESS_PROBE` | warning | no `readinessProbe` (regular containers only) |
+| `K8S_MISSING_LIVENESS_PROBE` | warning | no `livenessProbe` (regular containers only) |
+| `K8S_IMAGE_MISSING` | warning | no image |
+| `K8S_IMAGE_TAG_MISSING` | warning | image without a tag and without a digest |
+| `K8S_IMAGE_TAG_LATEST` | warning | image tagged `latest` |
+| `K8S_PRIVILEGED_CONTAINER` | error | `securityContext.privileged: true` |
+| `K8S_ALLOW_PRIVILEGE_ESCALATION_ENABLED` | error | `allowPrivilegeEscalation: true` |
+| `K8S_ALLOW_PRIVILEGE_ESCALATION_NOT_DISABLED` | warning | `allowPrivilegeEscalation` absent or not `false` |
+| `K8S_RUN_AS_NON_ROOT_NOT_SET` | warning | `runAsNonRoot: true` neither at pod nor at container level |
+
+Structural findings: `K8S_YAML_INVALID` (error), `K8S_DOCUMENT_NOT_A_MAPPING` (warning),
+`K8S_MISSING_KIND` (warning), `K8S_MISSING_POD_SPEC` (warning), `K8S_NO_CONTAINERS`
+(warning), `K8S_INVALID_CONTAINER` (warning).
+
+**Missing configuration is distinguished from explicitly risky configuration**: an
+absent `allowPrivilegeEscalation` is a *warning* (`..._NOT_DISABLED`), while an explicit
+`true` is an *error* (`..._ENABLED`). The same separation applies to `privileged`.
+
+**Init containers are handled separately**: they are inspected for image, resources and
+security context, but the readiness/liveness probe rules are **not** applied to them
+(probes are not meaningful for init containers).
+
+### Limitations
+
+These checks are a **narrow, static lint**, not a complete Kubernetes validation and not
+a security certification. In particular they do **not** cover: API version validity,
+schema conformance, admission policies, RBAC, NetworkPolicy, PodSecurity admission,
+seccomp/AppArmor profiles, capabilities, volume and secret handling, resource-quota
+fitness, or anything that requires a live cluster. A clean result means only that the
+listed checks found nothing.
+
+## Adding a new tool
+
+1. Create `app/toolbox/tools/<name>.py` with:
+   * a Pydantic `BaseModel` describing the tool input;
+   * a class with the attributes `name`, `version`, `description`, `input_model` and a
+     method `execute(self, params, limits) -> ToolResult`;
+   * a module-level `TOOL = YourTool()` instance.
+   The module must not import FastAPI: the analysis layer stays framework-independent.
+2. Import the module in `app/toolbox/tools/__init__.py` and add it to `__all__`.
+3. Add the instance to the tuple in `_register_builtin_tools()` in
+   `app/toolbox/registry.py`. Identifiers must be unique: `register()` raises
+   `ValueError` on a duplicate.
+4. Add unit tests under `tests/` and, if the tool needs a new bound, a
+   `toolbox_*` field in `app/config/settings.py` plus the corresponding field in
+   `ToolboxLimits`.
+
+There is no dynamic import, no `eval`/`exec`, no shell and no plugin discovery: the
+registry is a static list, so a client can never make the process load or run anything.
+
+## Architecture note
+
+* **Layering**: `app/toolbox/base.py` (protocol, enums, dataclasses, limits, controlled
+  exceptions) → `app/toolbox/tools/*` (pure analysis) → `app/toolbox/registry.py`
+  (explicit registry) → `app/models/toolbox.py` (Pydantic API models) →
+  `app/routers/toolbox.py` (HTTP only). The analysis functions import no FastAPI symbol
+  and are unit-tested directly.
+* **Execution model**: parsing is CPU-bound, so the router offloads `tool.execute` with
+  `loop.run_in_executor(None, ...)`, the same pattern already used by `/api/fib/{x}` in
+  `app/routers/api.py`. The event loop is never blocked by a large manifest.
+* **Observability**: no parallel stack. The router emits one loguru line per run with
+  `run_id`, `tool`, `status`, `outcome` and `duration_ms` — never the payload, the
+  manifest, the diff or a possible secret. Prometheus metrics come from the existing
+  `prometheus-fastapi-instrumentator` instrumentation of all routes, and tracing from
+  the existing OpenTelemetry FastAPI instrumentation. No custom metric with `run_id`,
+  payload or resource-name labels is added.
+* **Alternatives excluded**: a separate microservice (the mandate forbids it and it
+  would add deployment surface); a task queue (the checks are bounded and synchronous);
+  a persistence layer for runs (no durable store is needed, so no `GET /runs/{id}` was
+  added and no in-memory store pretends to be durable); a plugin system with dynamic
+  imports (a security risk with no MVP benefit); LLM agents (out of scope).
+* **Prefix**: `/api/toolbox` instead of the literal `/api/v1/toolbox`, because the
+  application has no versioned prefix anywhere and the mandate asks to adapt the prefix
+  to repository conventions.
